WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:03.940
A new observation of the bending of distant starlight

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:05.670
by gravity has allowed researchers

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:07.900
to weigh a white dwarf star,

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:11.640
virtually impossible before now for an isolated star.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:13.700
One of the key predictions of Einstein’s

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:15.480
general theory of relativity

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:18.420
is that the curvature of space near a massive body,

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:19.500
such as a star,

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:21.700
causes a ray of light passing near it

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:23.620
to be deflected by twice the amount

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:25.480
that would be expected.

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:27.240
So when a star passes between us

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:28.860
and a more distant star,

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:31.860
we should see the distant star shift position or --

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:33.680
in the case of perfect
alignment --

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:35.580
become distorted into a perfectly

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:37.020
circular ring of light –

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:40.080
a so-called “Einstein
ring.”

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:41.940
One of the first convincing proofs

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:43.420
of general relativity

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:46.420
came in 1919 during an eclipse of the sun

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:48.280
when astronomers saw its gravity

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:49.860
shift the position of stars

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:51.340
close to its edge.

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:52.780
In recent decades,

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:55.160
astronomers have seen the gravity of galaxies

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:58.020
produce Einstein rings of more distant galaxies,

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:01.240
and stars briefly brighten the image of other stars

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:05.040
in a process known as gravitational microlensing,

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:07.400
but detecting a position shift caused by a star

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:11.040
other than the sun has been too small to discern.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:13.940
Seeing such a shift would be very useful to
astronomers

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:15.600
because it would provide a way to

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.300
to directly measure the mass of the intervening star,

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.380
something that is very hard to do unless

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:22.380
a star is in a binary pair

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:25.440
when their orbits reveal their mass.

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.280
Recently, researchers realized

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:28.380
that the keen eyesight of the

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:30.160
Hubble Space Telescope might

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:31.880
be able to spot such movement

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:33.480
so went looking for stars

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:35.900
that might soon be in alignment.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:39.940
They found that the nearby white dwarf Stein 2051 B

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:41.680
was set to pass nearly in front of

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:44.920
another star in March of 2014.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:47.140
They then used Hubble to measure tiny shifts

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:48.680
in the apparent position of the background

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:52.640
star as Stein 2051 B passed by.

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:53.840
The researchers’ measurements

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:55.680
put the white dwarf star’s mass

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:59.080
to be about 0.675 solar masses.

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:00.580
As well as demonstrating the power

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:01.820
of this new technique,

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:03.540
the work also solved an enigma

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:06.340
surrounding Stein 2051 B.

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:07.860
An earlier mass estimate had made it

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:10.860
substantially lighter, too light for a white dwarf

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:13.620
of that type, casting doubt on the theory

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:16.480
of how such burnt-out stellar remnants form.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:19.720
But the new measurement puts Stein 2051 B’s

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:22.420
mass exactly where it should be.

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:23.880
With this one measurement,

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:26.840
the researchers have confirmed Einstein’s prediction,

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:29.160
demonstrated a valuable new technique,

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:32.400
and reinforced the theory of stellar evolution.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.000
That's a good day’s work.

